WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.470
<v Instructor>Next question.</v>

2
00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.680
So a company has deployed a multi-tier web application

3
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.630
in the AWS Cloud.

4
00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.910
The application consists of the following tiers.

5
00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.400
We have a Windows-based web tier

6
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.440
that is hosted on Amazon EC2 instances

7
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.300
with Elastic IP addresses,

8
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.670
with have a Linux-based application tier

9
00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.830
that is hosted on Amazon EC2 instances

10
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.790
behind the ALB that uses path-based routing

11
00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.440
and then a MySQL database that runs

12
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.630
on the Linux EC2 instance.

13
00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.630
So all the EC2 instances are using the Intel-based x86 CPUs

14
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.010
and we need to modernize the infrastructure

15
00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.200
to achieve better performance.

16
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.680
Overall, we want to also minimize the operational overhead

17
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:41.910
of the application.

18
00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.040
So better performance, less overhead,

19
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.050
and modernized infrastructure.

20
00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.750
So which actions should we do?

21
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:49.770
So let's have a look at the options.

22
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.330
So first of all, the architecture.

23
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:55.260
We have the web tier with Windows Instances and elastic IPs.

24
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.690
As you know, this is bad just to run elastic IPs

25
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.390
on many EC2 instances for a tier.

26
00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.650
The application tier looks better

27
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.990
because while we have an application load balancer

28
00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.750
connected to EC2 instances, so there's only one entry point

29
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.940
into our application, which is great,

30
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.390
and the database tier is you're using MySQL on EC2

31
00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.950
and we know that's horrible.

32
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:15.990
We need to reuse a managed service

33
00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.360
to reduce our management overhead.

34
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.000
So option one is to run the MySQL database

35
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.270
on multiple EC2 instances just like that.

36
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.940
So this is going to increase maybe our availability

37
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.620
but also increase our headaches

38
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:30.810
which is not what we want from this question.

39
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.970
Then to migrate a MySQL database

40
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.190
into an Amazon or Aurora Serverless

41
00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.750
so that's completely possible,

42
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.590
Aurora as an API for MySQL

43
00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.840
and Aurora Serverless is a very fine database.

44
00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.220
It's actually serverless,

45
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.110
so we have no management overhead

46
00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.790
and incredible performance.

47
00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.440
So this seems like a good choice.

48
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.280
And then, migrates all EC2 instances types to Graviton2.

49
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.940
While it turns out that we can do this,

50
00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.860
Graviton2 will provide us better performance

51
00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.550
because this is a newer kind of CPU architecture

52
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.600
but this is not available at all for Windows Instances.

53
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.080
So because we have the web tier using Windows Instances,

54
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.690
then this is not an acceptable solution.

55
00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.870
But this would be if everything was using Linux.

56
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.340
So the correct answer

57
00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.673
in this option group is going to be answer C.

58
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.520
If we look at the option group two,

59
00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.400
we have the option to place the web tier instances

60
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.040
behind an ALB, which will look like this.

61
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.840
And the applicational balancer

62
00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.140
for sure works with any kind of web application.

63
00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.080
So we could definitely have Windows EC2 instances

64
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:41.190
behind an ALB and this looks very modern and scalable,

65
00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.830
whereas, the option is to replace ALB

66
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.220
for the app tier with a company managed load balancer.

67
00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.560
So I don't know what the company load balancer

68
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.550
would look like, this is anti-pattern for AWS.

69
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:56.700
This seems like a very actually easy answer to rule out.

70
00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.393
So, of course, in this example, the correct answer is B.

71
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.880
And that's it for this question.

72
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.830
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

